WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.800
 There was this whole world of coders and hardware guys in the '60s, and I thought to myself,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.040
 I bet there's a really interesting story here.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.520
 So my editor at Simon & Schuster was totally 100% behind it.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.180
 The working title was really bad.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:27.720
 It was called Building Cyberspace, so we knew we needed a new title.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.500
 And Matt, my late husband, didn't come in on the project.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.060
 He was actually a better writer than I am and a wonderful, just had an absolutely incredible

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.880
 mind and could grasp anything.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:47.060
 And he didn't come in on the project until about maybe a year into it.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:57.400
 When I was getting overwhelmed, I had this new baby, and I was working at Newsweek, and

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.680
 I realized that there was just more to the story than I could do myself, it would have

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.160
 taken me like double the time.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:11.520
 So that's when he came in on the project, and I found, indeed, that there was this incredible

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.000
 untold story of not so much the Internet, so we have to keep it really clear, the ARPANET

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.960
 and how that started, and in fact, Matt's the one who wrote the chapter, The Fastest

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.000
 Million Dollars, which is about how it got funded at IPTO, the Information Processing

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.680
 Techniques Office at what was then DARPA and then became ARPANET, DARPA and then ARPA again.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:47.000
 These people were all, as I had hoped, incredibly interesting, really smart, and had this new

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.540
 idea for how computers could talk to each other.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:02:03.360
 One of the most amazing things was visiting Larry Roberts, who was running IPTO at the

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.160
 time that this whole thing was drawn out.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.860
 We were at his house in Woodside, and we went out into the garage.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:15.120
 This is how un-researched this topic was, because, you know, who'd have thunk, right?

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.920
 And we go out into his garage, and there are all these boxes of old, mildewy papers, and

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:28.360
 he starts pulling them out, and there are old letters between him and people at MIT,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.400
 and then he pulls out this amazing set of sketches of him.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.720
 It's like, remember when Trudeau Doonesbury did Inside Reagan's Brain?

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.560
 This was like inside Larry Roberts' brain.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:49.540
 He was just sketching out all the possible configurations of what this network could

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.540
 look like.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:57.400
 I've got these pictures in the book, which you can show, but here, you know, these are

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:03.920
 all his very early diagrams, just sort of popping out of his head.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:11.000
 This is the early notes on the ARPANET, and I just loved finding all this incredible primary

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.600
 source material, and I felt like I should have been paying somebody to be able to do

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.600
 this rather than…

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.440
 Right, because it's just in a garage that millions of people drive by, right, and it's

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.240
 all of a sudden this important artifact of what we're living right now.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.760
 Right, and we were able to sort of, you know, capture it, and Bolt, Baranek, and Newman

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.360
 was a big force behind the book.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.760
 The book was in part their idea, and so these guys, because what we tend to forget is that

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:49.400
 at BBN, they built the first imp and the interface message processor.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.720
 In fact, there's a very funny story in the book where Ted Kennedy, you know, Senator

00:03:53.720 --> 00:04:00.240
 from Massachusetts, sends a letter to BBN congratulating them on getting the contract

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.040
 for the interfaith message processor.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.040
 I know.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.640
 So, a completely uncharted territory, and they had to build this piece of hardware,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.520
 which they did, and program it, which they did a whole new thing, and it was…

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.320
 So I spent a lot of time with the folks at BBN, got to know them well, and that group,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:27.600
 they were completely on school in talking to, you know, reporters or a book researcher,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.240
 and there was this one guy, Ray Tomlinson, everybody said, "Oh, you should go talk

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.200
 to Ray because he came up with the @ sign."

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.200
 I said, "Oh, really?

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:43.880
 Okay," and so, you know, there's this wonderful, really quiet guy, Ray Tomlinson,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.880
 sitting in his office, and I knocked on his door, I said, "I understand you came up

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:48.880
 with the @ sign."

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.720
 He goes, "Oh, yeah, yeah," and then he explains to me, you know, how it is that he

00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:00.000
 did this, and then, you know, and now it's a huge legend that Ray Tomlinson did this,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.760
 and the software guys, really…

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:10.040
 So, I have this image of you sort of just driving and driving and going and going back

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.040
 and forth.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.480
 I mean, how long was the research?

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.480
 How long did you research this?

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.480
 Was this like…

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.440
 I think, yeah, the research took a couple of years, and it wasn't so much driving.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.400
 The hacker book was like driving all over, all over the place, because that was three

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.400
 different stories of three different hackers.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.600
 This was more, in fact, email between the hacker book and this book and Wizards, so

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:39.280
 1988, and like when I started this book, 1993, email had come, it was much more popular,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.040
 so there were a lot of…we did a lot of emailing back and forth.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.040
 I spent a lot of time…I did go from…I was living in Austin at the time.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:54.460
 I did go from Austin to Boston a lot, and I spent a lot of time, actually, I did fly

00:05:54.460 --> 00:06:04.560
 to L.A. and spent a lot of time with John Postel, who came up with the domain name system,

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:12.680
 and he died in '90…I want to say '98, '99, and that was shocking.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.040
 I spent a lot of time with him.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.240
 Tell me about…tell me about John.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.360
 Well, John…so anyone who's ever seen a picture of John, maybe you have one you can

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.920
 dig up, this very long, kind of Santa Claus-like beard, and what's amazing is I spent a lot

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:27.920
 of time at his house.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:35.600
 He lived in a teeny, tiny little house in the L.A. area with his girlfriend, this very

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:42.280
 nice woman named Susan, and on the refrigerator, there were all these family photos, and they

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:48.800
 all…they were all like these people with long beards, long white beards, what an interesting

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:56.320
 family, and he drove, I think, this kind of beat-up Volvo or something like that, something

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:07.480
 completely unpretentious, and lived so unpretentiously, and had all his files right in his study in

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:14.760
 this little house, all this history, and also at ISI, where he worked in Marina del Rey,

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:21.640
 and we spent hours and hours and hours together, first in Boston, where we were both at an

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:28.000
 anniversary party of the ARPANET, and we spent a lot of time together, and I thought, you

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.560
 know, this guy is key, and he was so quiet that sort of getting him to open up really

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:38.520
 meant spending a lot of time with him, and he was…oh, and we met at one of the very

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.840
 first INAC conferences in 1995 in Hawaii and spent a lot of time there, and he just was

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:49.400
 so patiently trying to explain to me things, but I'll never forget this one thing that

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.560
 happened with him in Hawaii at the INAC conference, it's the Internet Society Conference, one

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:02.760
 of the very early ones, and I just was asking him some questions, and he goes…he looked

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.080
 completely disturbed and distracted, and he said, "No, no, there's someone over across

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:12.680
 the room I have to talk to," and it was someone from a very small country, sort of

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:18.360
 wanting to talk to John about their domain, the country's domain name, or something,

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:26.120
 but to John, it was far more important that he deal with this person's problem than cement

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:28.760
 his place in history, in this ARPANET history.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:34.880
 In fact, when I was working on the book, I sent him an email, I'll never forget this,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:40.360
 I think I don't have the email anymore, and I said, "Just out of curiosity, why haven't

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:46.640
 you ever wanted to get rich," because that's when people were starting to get rich, so

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:55.160
 this was in '95, and he said, "It's just not what this is about," isn't that a wonderful

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.160
 thing?

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.160
 It is.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.160
 Yeah, and that's who John was.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.760
 One of the things that I've been curious about is how this got by AT&T, how this made

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.080
 it past AT&T.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:18.680
 I know, talk about No Vision, well, Paul Barron, he couldn't convince AT&T to build the very

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:25.280
 marvelous network that he designed, and then these other guys, the next guys, the next

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:33.760
 crew that came in, and they were very, very, think about it back then, they had a monopoly,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.360
 what did they really care?

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:51.120
 They had the Bell Research Labs, and it was a not-invented-here kind of syndrome, and

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.480
 they didn't see it, I mean, talk about No Vision, they simply didn't see it, but I think

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:03.920
 the fact, the not-invented-here part of it was like, "What would we possibly need this

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:04.920
 for?"

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.640
 They were in a context.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:12.240
 They were in their own context, yeah, but thank goodness, I mean, thank goodness they

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:18.120
 didn't get their hands on this thing, the same with IBM, sorry, you know, just, we didn't

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:26.960
 want corporate America, or DEC for that matter, the original hardware was based on a Honeywell

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.760
 machine, and thank goodness Honeywell doesn't exist anymore.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:36.680
 It sounds terrible that I'm saying this, but it's, but it was all kind of happy, accidental

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.320
 sort of coincidences.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:46.520
 I think Al Gore is- Oh, totally underestimated and underappreciated, is that what you were

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:47.520
 going to say?

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.520
 Yeah.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:49.520
 Oh, yeah.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:50.520
 I mean, why mock him?

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:58.040
 Every time, every time I tell people I wrote a history of the origins of the internet,

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.920
 people say very mockingly, "Oh, did Al Gore invent it?"

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.440
 And I won't play into that.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:10.960
 He played a huge role in terms of policy and direction.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:18.360
 And when Clinton was running for president, you know, they were out there with this very

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:29.160
 important technology white paper that, I mean, you can't do things like this unless you have

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.760
 the support that people like Al Gore provided.

